# Tidyverse Style for New Code

When adding or editing R code, default to tidyverse conventions.

- Use pipes (`|>`) for multi-step data workflows.
- Prefer `dplyr` verbs (`select`, `filter`, `mutate`, `summarise`, `group_by`, `arrange`) over base R equivalents in pipeline code.
- Use `tidyr` for reshaping (`pivot_longer`, `pivot_wider`) and be explicit about IDs/fills when needed.
- Keep one transformation per line in pipelines for readability.
- Use explicit `na.rm = TRUE` in summaries where missing values may occur.
- End grouped operations with `.groups = "drop"` unless grouping should persist.
- Prefer descriptive, snake_case object names.

## Example

```r
# Good
dat_summary <- dat |>
  filter(!is.na(value)) |>
  group_by(siteID, treatment) |>
  summarise(mean_value = mean(value, na.rm = TRUE), .groups = "drop")
```

```r
# Avoid for new workflow code
dat_summary <- aggregate(value ~ siteID + treatment, dat, mean)
```
